class PartitiveMember {
  definition {
    One member of a PartitiveRelation — a partitive concept plus
    ISO 704:2022 presence, count, and delimiting metadata.

    Mirrors ConceptSystemMember shape exactly. Kept as a distinct
    leaf class for type safety and to leave room for partitive-
    specific extensions if needed.

    Three orthogonal dimensions (MECE):
      presence      — required (default) | optional
      count         — exactly_one (default) | at_least_one | multiple
      is_delimiting — false (default) | true

    The combination (optional, at_least_one) is invalid: if a part
    is optional, the at-least-one constraint is vacuous and collapses
    to (optional, multiple).
  }
  +ref: <<StandardDocument>> ReferenceToTermbase [1] {
    definition {
      The subordinate concept partitive — the part. Encoded as a
      ConceptRef (source + id, optional text), same shape as
      RelatedConcept#ref.
    }
  }
  +presence: PartitivePresence [0..1] {
    definition {
      Whether this partitive must exist in every instance
      (required) or may exist only in some instances (optional).

      Defaults to required when omitted. ISO 704:2022 encodes
      this as the line style: solid (required) vs dashed (optional).
    }
  }
  +count: PartitiveCount [0..1] {
    definition {
      How many instances of this partitive exist. ISO 704:2022
      encodes this via the number of lines.

      Defaults to exactly_one when omitted. The combination
      presence: optional + count: at_least_one is invalid and
      collapses to (optional, multiple).
    }
  }
  +is_delimiting: Boolean [0..1] {
    definition {
      ISO 704:2022 delimiting part (bold 3x-width line in diagram).
      When true, this part behaves like a delimiting characteristic:
      it distinguishes the comprehensive from coordinate concepts.

      Example (ISO 704 §5.5.4.2.2): for "optomechanical mouse", the
      delimiting parts are mouse ball, x/y-axis rollers, and IR
      emitter/sensor — they distinguish it from "mechanical mouse"
      and "optical mouse". Mouse button is NOT delimiting (all
      computer mice have buttons).

      Orthogonal to presence and count. Defaults to false when omitted.
    }
  }
}
